    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail on the feedback loop aspect? Like, how exactly does the drop in Angiotensin II affect the renin levels specifically? I want to make sure I have the whole cycle down for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The resulting decrease in angiotensin II weakens the negative feedback signal that normally stimulates juxtaglomerular cells in the kidney to release renin. This disruption causes a compensatory auto-increase in plasma renin activity, a phenomenon known to be highly characteristic of ACE inhibitor therapy. This elevated renin level represents the body's attempt to restore the RAAS cycle, making it a key identifier for ACE inhibitor use in diagnostic scenarios.",
      "source": "target",
