    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it, so the renin goes up because the feedback is gone. That's a lot of moving parts. Can you summarize the whole process from the initial renin release to the final drop in blood pressure in a way that's easy to memorize? Like a step-by-step flow? Thanks!",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors block the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II, which is the primary driver of vasoconstriction in this pathway. Simultaneously, the spike in renin leads to higher levels of Angiotensin I, but without activation, it cannot create the vasoconstrictive effect needed to maintain blood pressure. Additionally, reduced Angiotensin II signaling decreases aldosterone, causing the kidneys to excrete more sodium and water. This combination of decreased vascular tone and reduced blood volume lowers systemic resistance and pressure.",
      "source": "target",
